          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"The firmament most commonly symbolizes the created heavens as a visible witness to God’s glory, sovereignty, and ordering of creation.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Genesis 1:6–8, 14–18 — God creates and orders the firmament, naming it heaven and placing the heavenly lights within it.\",\"Psalm 19:1 — “the firmament” proclaims God’s handiwork.\",\"Psalm 150:1 — God is praised in “the firmament of his power.”\",\"Daniel 12:3 — the brightness of the firmament supplies an image for the glory of the righteous.\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"The firmament is primarily a cosmological feature rather than a symbol: the expanse or dome separating the waters above from those below.\",\"basis\":[\"Genesis 1:6–8\",\"Genesis 1:14–17\",\"Genesis 7:11\",\"Ezekiel 1:22–26\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"The firmament refers to the ordinary visible sky or atmosphere, including the region in which birds fly and weather occurs.\",\"basis\":[\"Genesis 1:20\",\"Psalm 78:23–24\",\"Psalm 148:4\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"The firmament denotes heaven or the celestial realm and therefore represents divine transcendence, authority, or the boundary between earthly and heavenly realities.\",\"basis\":[\"Genesis 1:8\",\"Ezekiel 1:22–28\",\"Psalm 150:1\",\"Revelation 4:6\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"The firmament represents separation, boundaries, and the establishment of order out of primordial chaos.\",\"basis\":[\"Genesis 1:2, 6–8\",\"Psalm 104:2–9\",\"Proverbs 8:27–29\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"In some patristic and medieval allegories, the firmament signifies the Church, Scripture, spiritual knowledge, or steadfast believers who separate higher spiritual realities from lower earthly ones.\",\"basis\":[\"Genesis 1:6–8\",\"Daniel 12:3\",\"Interpretive associations with the apostles, teachers, or saints as heavenly lights\"],\"prominence\":\"rare\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":false,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"There is no settled, uniquely Christian symbolic meaning of “firmament.” Its basic biblical function is cosmological, while its most recognizable symbolic use—especially from Psalm 19:1—is as the created heavens bearing witness to God’s glory and ordered power. Interpretations also vary according to whether the underlying Hebrew raqia is understood as a solid vault, an expanse, the atmosphere, outer space, or heaven.\"}"
        }
      ],
